  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nA few thunderstorms pulsed through the region this morning, but \nstruggled with strong low level shear essentially tearing them \napart. CAMs continue to allow weak showers and storms to bubble up \nin the moist, unstable airmass though the afternoon. Temperatures \nhave struggled to reach the low to mid 80s by noon today, given the \nlingering morning cloud cover. Outside of the clouds, some sites \nhave pushed into the upper 80s and low 90s. Temperatures are \nexpected to continue to climb into the low to mid 90s across the \nforecast area this afternoon, aided by strong southwesterly flow. \nHeat indices could reach as high as 100-105 in far southeast \nNebraska and southwest Iowa. Exactly how warm we get will be greatly \ndependent on how much cloud cover lingers through the \nafternoon.\n\nAnother round of showers and storms may develop late tonight/early \nMonday morning, in the warm airmass ahead of a weak cold front \ndrifting in from the northeast. While a strong wind gust or two may \nbe possible, widespread severe weather looks unlikely. \n\nMonday and Beyond...\n\nThe upper ridge that brought this weekend's heat will slip to the \neast Monday, with flatter southwesterly flow overspreading the \nregion. Temperatures will still remain well above average, with \nhighs topping out in the upper 80s and 90s through the upcoming \nweek. \n\nA weak front will linger over the forecast area Monday, providing a \npotential impetus for convective initiation. However, recent CAM \nruns have been waffling on whether or not strong updrafts will form. \nIf storms are able to develop, steep midlevel lapse rates, up to 30-\n40 kts of 0-6km shear, and 1000-2000 J/kg of instability would bring \nthe potential for a few severe storms capable of producing large \nhail and damaging wind gusts.  \n\nMultiple shortwaves rippling through the region will bring repeated \nchances for showers and storms through the upcoming week. \nUnfortunately, widespread precipitation doesn't look like a slam \ndunk, as upper ridging will gradually push back into the Central \nPlains from the west. Overall daily precip chances will remain \naround 10-30%, with occasional isolated strong storms possible.",
